      ["Perform serial therapeutic hyperosmolar water-soluble contrast enemas under fluoroscopic guidance", "The infant has meconium ileus, the pathognomonic presenting gastrointestinal manifestation of cystic fibrosis in up to 20% of newborns, caused by thick, dehydrated, viscous meconium obstructing the terminal ileum. Key radiographic features include: (1) small bowel dilation with Neuhauser 'soap bubble' sign (gas mixed with thick meconium), (2) absence of rectal 'blast sign' (ruling out Hirschsprung disease), and (3) a 'microcolon' on contrast enema (the colon is hypoplastic because normal meconium never traversed it during gestation). In uncomplicated meconium ileus (no volvulus, atresia, or perforation), a therapeutic hyperosmolar water-soluble contrast enema (Gastrografin) is the established first-line treatment: the hyperosmolar solution draws fluid into the bowel lumen, softening and hydrating the inspissated meconium plugs to facilitate their spontaneous evacuation in > 70% of uncomplicated cases, avoiding laparotomy."],
